recommendations.recommendation:
Send the fab one archive per board, holding one set of fabrication data in a single format, all exported from the same revision of the design:
- one Gerber file per layer (every copper layer, both solder masks, both legends, both paste layers), in Gerber X2 if your tool supports it;
- drill files (see [PCB-DFM-011](https://opendfx.org/01-design-package/01-fabrication-data/#pcb-dfm-011-separate-plated-and-non-plated-holes-and-use-an-unambiguous-drill-format));
- the board outline as its own file;
- an IPC-D-356 netlist (see [PCB-DFM-009](https://opendfx.org/01-design-package/01-fabrication-data/#pcb-dfm-009-always-send-a-netlist-made-from-the-same-revision-as-the-gerbers));
- a fabrication drawing, plus the same specifications in a plain-text file (see [PCB-DFM-014](https://opendfx.org/01-design-package/01-fabrication-data/#pcb-dfm-014-put-everything-the-gerbers-cant-say-on-the-fabrication-drawing)).

ODB++ or IPC-2581 can replace the Gerbers, drill files and netlist, if your fab says it reads them. Send one format or the other, not both.

Keep your native design files and schematic out of the fab package. Send them to your assembler, and to the fab only if asked, in a separate folder marked "reference only" (see [PCB-DFA-064](https://opendfx.org/01-design-package/02-assembly-data/#pcb-dfa-064-always-send-the-xy-file-and-send-native-design-files-and-the-schematic-when-you-can)).

Give every board a fabrication drawing that states, at least:
- performance class (IPC-6012 Class 2 or Class 3);
- base material, called out by grade and Tg (for example against an IPC-4101 slash sheet), not just "FR-4", and finished board thickness and its tolerance;
- copper weight for each layer;
- stack-up, and any controlled impedance as a table: structure, layers, target impedance, tolerance and reference planes;
- surface finish, solder mask color and legend color;
- via treatment (tented, plugged, filled);
- board outline with critical dimensions, cut-outs and slots, and the drill chart with tolerances;
- revision, date and author.

Put the same specifications in a plain-text file in the package too. If you order through a web form, make sure it matches the drawing.

Send a placement file (also called an XY, centroid or pick-and-place file) with every assembly order.
List every part location on the board, on both sides, with:
- reference designator (exactly as in the BOM);
- X and Y of the part's center, in mm;
- rotation in degrees;
- side (top or bottom);
- whether it's surface-mount or through-hole.

Keep do-not-place locations in the file; whether a location is fitted belongs in the BOM ([PCB-DFA-071](https://opendfx.org/01-design-package/03-bom/#pcb-dfa-071-mark-do-not-place-and-customer-supplied-lines-clearly)). Also send your native design files and the schematic, in a separate folder marked "reference only" (see [PCB-DFM-008](https://opendfx.org/01-design-package/01-fabrication-data/#pcb-dfm-008-send-one-complete-fabrication-package-in-one-format-from-one-revision)).

Add an assembly drawing, or a short marked-up image, for anything the XY file and BOM can't express:
- through-hole leads that must be bent, with the angle and the direction;
- screws, nuts and other hardware, with a torque value;
- parts that must sit at a set height or stand-off, or be glued, sleeved or potted;
- areas to keep clear of coating, glue or labels;
- any special process or material, specified by what it must do rather than by brand.

Give each line of the BOM at least:
- the reference designators it covers (matching the XY file and the legend exactly);
- the quantity per board;
- the manufacturer and the manufacturer's full part number, including any suffix for packaging, tolerance or termination;
- a short description (value, tolerance, voltage, package).

Useful extras: a line number (handy for listing alternates) and your own part number.

Send it as CSV or a spreadsheet. Use the part number exactly as the maker publishes it, the same in every document, and don't add commas or other characters to it.

For every controlled-impedance requirement, state on the fabrication drawing:
- the structure (for example, surface microstrip, embedded stripline, edge-coupled differential pair, coplanar waveguide with ground);
- the layer the trace is on and the layers it references;
- the target impedance and its tolerance;
- the trace width and spacing you designed with.

Give impedance traces an unusual width, such as 0.159 mm (6.25 mil) instead of 0.15 mm, so the fab can find them among all the other traces. Leave space beside them, so the fab can widen them if their materials need it. Calculate your own design to within about 1% of the target before you send it.

Design with plating in mind:
- give finished hole sizes ([PCB-DFM-023](https://opendfx.org/03-bare-board-fabrication/03-drilling/#pcb-dfm-023-give-hole-sizes-as-finished-sizes));
- set outer-layer trace and space rules for the plated copper thickness, not the starting foil ([PCB-DFM-020](https://opendfx.org/03-bare-board-fabrication/01-materials-and-stack-up/#pcb-dfm-020-size-traces-and-gaps-for-the-finished-copper-weight));
- keep the aspect ratio within what plates well ([PCB-DFM-024](https://opendfx.org/03-bare-board-fabrication/03-drilling/#pcb-dfm-024-keep-the-aspect-ratio-at-81-or-less-and-holes-025-mm-or-larger-where-you-can));
- if you need more copper in the holes than the standard, say so on the drawing.

When you choose a part, check three things as well as its electrical fit:
- its lifecycle status is active (not "not recommended for new designs", last-time-buy or obsolete);
- several authorized distributors stock it in quantity;
- for anything but a truly special part, an equivalent exists from another maker.

Check these when you choose the part, not only when you release the BOM ([PCB-DFA-070](https://opendfx.org/01-design-package/03-bom/)). Before moving from prototype to production, look again at any part that was easy to find in tens but may not be in thousands.

If you hold moisture-sensitive parts yourself:
- after taking parts out, reseal the bag within the hour with fresh desiccant and a humidity indicator card, ideally with a heat sealer;
- push the air out, but don't vacuum-seal tight;
- use bags made for electronics that block both moisture and static;
- if a bag stays open longer than an hour, write on it the date and time it was opened, and how long it was out;
- keep opened bags from being punctured (tube ends and sharp reel edges are the usual cause).

If the humidity card shows the parts have been exposed, don't bake them yourself: send them as they are and tell your assembler.

When a part can only be found outside authorized channels:
- ask the seller for proof the parts came from the maker (an invoice, delivery note or goods-received record tied to the lot);
- ask whether they hold the stock themselves or are offering someone else's;
- have the parts inspected, and tested if they're costly or critical;
- build a few boards with them and check they work before committing the whole order.

Send surface-mount parts in a form a machine can feed:
- reels, or cut tape in one continuous length per part number, not many short strips;
- no cut strip shorter than 50 mm, and preferably 150 mm or more;
- tubes only as a last resort, after reels, cut tape and trays are ruled out, and then with every part facing the same way;
- trays, or protective foam for tray parts, never loose in a bag.

Through-hole and hardware parts can come in labeled bags.
